.SH NAME
chkwhat \- print Zilog Source Control what strings
.SH SYNOPSIS
chkwhat [ -w ] \fIfile \fR...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Chkwhat
searches the specified files for "what strings"
and lists the identifying portion of these strings.
If the
.I \-w
option is used the entire "what string" will be printed.
This option can be helpful when placing "what strings" in an archive.
The file need not be a text file.
.LP
A "what string" shows which version of a source file
the specified file is or is associated with.
It is defined as beginning with the four-character
sequence @[$] and ending with a null, newline, ", or '.
Chkwhat lists the string from after the @[$] to before
the terminating character.
.SH SEE ALSO
zsc(5), chkin(1), chkout(1)
.SH LIMITATIONS
The "what string" was presumably created when the file
or the source code it was generated from
was properly checked out of a Zilog Source Control
file, but there is no guarantee of this.
